.TH MONOAUTH 8
.SH NAME
monoauth \- authenticated, encrypted remote execution with Monocypher
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B listen \-a mono
.I addr command ...
.br
.B rcmd \-e mono
.I addr command ...
.br
.B cpu \-C mono
.I mach command ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mono
is an authentication and transport algorithm for
.IR listen (1),
.IR rcmd (1)
and
.IR cpu (1).
It is chosen by name instead of the keyring/SSL algorithms
.RB ( none ,
.BR rc4_128 ,
\&...).
The key material comes from
.IR certify (8):
a signer key in
.BR /keydb/mono-signer ,
and one identity file per user,
.BR /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/mono ,
holding a name, a 64-byte EdDSA secret key and a 64-byte certificate.
.PP
Both sides send one message:
.IP
.EX
len name || name || pk || cert || ephemeral pk
.EE
.IP
The certificate is an EdDSA signature over
.IR name || "\en" || pk ,
verified with the signer's public key (bytes 32 to 64 of
.BR mono-signer ).
The ephemeral X25519 keys give a shared secret; BLAKE2b derives one key
per direction from a transcript of both messages plus the shared
secret; XChaCha20-Poly1305 protects each record, with a per-direction
counter as the nonce.
.PP
Records are a 4-byte length, a 16-byte tag and the ciphertext.  The
plaintext side is a file-channel stream, so callers get one file
descriptor they can read, write and pass to
.IR export (2).
.PP
The authenticated name is passed to the command as
.B $user
in the
.IR listen (1)
context, exactly as with the keyring algorithms.
.SH FILES
.TF /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/mono
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer
EdDSA signer key (private half; root only)
.TP
.BI /usr/ name /keyring/mono
name, secret key and certificate
.TP
.B /tmp/mono
file-channel directory for the plaintext side
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/monoauth.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mono (2),
.IR certify (8),
.IR listen (1),
.IR rcmd (1),
.IR cpu (1)
.SH BUGS
The transport is experimental.  A single authenticated session works
end to end, including a session run as a different user, but a second
session on the same listener hangs: mounting and exporting the
file-channel descriptor appears to leak state that wedges the namespace.
Do not use
.B \-a mono
on a service until that is replaced by a kernel device in the style of
.BR devssl .
.PP
There is no forward secrecy beyond the ephemeral exchange, no
revocation, and no rekeying; the nonce counter space is 2 to the 32.
